    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A concubine who could read and write Chinese, Cixi allied with Empress Dowager Ci'an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> in 1861 to oust the eight regents appointed by the dying Xianfeng Emperor, and became the only Qing empress dowager to rule as regent.</li><li>She backed the Tongzhi Restoration and the learning of Western technology, but stopped the railway and the study abroad programme at times, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a> in 1898 and had six reformers beheaded.</li><li>Her record is disputed: she has long been portrayed as a ruthless reactionary, while revisionist historians argue she was scapegoated for structural problems, and one of her best-known sources, Backhouse, was later found to have forged much of his material.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Cixi's physical remains were disturbed in 1928, when the troops of the warlord Sun Dianying dynamited their way into her mausoleum and tore her jewels from her body.</p></aside>
    </section>

